1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,020
Zoya now has three kinds of customers.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:05,158
Gym-goers want more protein.

3
00:00:05,158 --> 00:00:07,774
Office workers want a quick desk snack.

4
00:00:07,774 --> 00:00:10,800
Parents want a healthy
sweet for tiffin boxes.

5
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:13,482
One small kitchen can't make all of it.

6
00:00:13,482 --> 00:00:15,079
So whom should she serve?

7
00:00:15,729 --> 00:00:18,714
In the last few episodes,
Zoya did her homework.

8
00:00:19,064 --> 00:00:22,769
She asked people at school
gates, at gyms, and at offices.

9
00:00:23,119 --> 00:00:25,106
Then she took three big steps.

10
00:00:25,106 --> 00:00:26,312
Split the market.

11
00:00:26,312 --> 00:00:27,424
Pick one group.

12
00:00:27,424 --> 00:00:29,120
Stand out in their minds.

13
00:00:29,470 --> 00:00:32,382
Marketers call these three steps S, T, P.

14
00:00:33,032 --> 00:00:34,630
So what is STP?

15
00:00:34,930 --> 00:00:38,131
It stands for segmentation,
targeting and positioning.

16
00:00:38,481 --> 00:00:42,727
Segmentation means splitting a market
into groups of buyers with similar

17
00:00:42,727 --> 00:00:43,627
needs.

18
00:00:43,678 --> 00:00:46,903
Targeting means choosing which
group, or groups, to serve.

19
00:00:47,253 --> 00:00:51,320
Positioning means owning a clear,
different place in that group's mind.

20
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:55,004
Kotler and Armstrong sum up
strategy in two questions.

21
00:00:55,004 --> 00:00:56,379
Whom will we serve?

22
00:00:56,379 --> 00:00:58,500
And how can we serve them best?

23
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:01,138
The ideas are not new.

24
00:01:01,138 --> 00:01:05,895
Wendell Smith wrote about
market segmentation in 1956.

25
00:01:05,895 --> 00:01:10,888
Al Ries and Jack Trout made
positioning famous in 1972.

26
00:01:11,538 --> 00:01:14,185
Now let's watch the whole flow, in one go.

27
00:01:14,535 --> 00:01:15,936
Step one, segment.

28
00:01:15,936 --> 00:01:18,906
Zoya's survey asked a
hundred and fifty people.

29
00:01:19,256 --> 00:01:21,383
She sorts them by what they need.

30
00:01:21,383 --> 00:01:24,235
Tiffin parents, gym-goers,
and office snackers.

31
00:01:24,585 --> 00:01:25,922
Step two, target.

32
00:01:25,922 --> 00:01:27,463
She scores each group.

33
00:01:27,463 --> 00:01:28,616
How big is it?

34
00:01:28,616 --> 00:01:29,663
Will it grow?

35
00:01:29,663 --> 00:01:31,213
Can we serve it well?

36
00:01:31,563 --> 00:01:32,463
Parents win.

37
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:36,388
There are many of them, they buy every
week, and small laddoos fit their

38
00:01:36,388 --> 00:01:37,288
need.

39
00:01:37,215 --> 00:01:41,327
Gym-goers want protein, and Nani's
recipe is not built for that.

40
00:01:41,327 --> 00:01:43,078
So they wait, for now.

41
00:01:43,428 --> 00:01:44,780
Step three, position.

42
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:47,808
When parents hear Laddoo
Lab, what should they think?

43
00:01:48,158 --> 00:01:50,437
The healthy sweet for tiffin boxes.

44
00:01:50,437 --> 00:01:52,957
One clear customer, one clear promise.

45
00:01:53,607 --> 00:01:56,427
Try to serve everyone,
and you serve no one well.

46
00:01:57,127 --> 00:01:59,397
How do you find a place no one owns?

47
00:01:59,397 --> 00:02:01,658
Draw a map of what buyers care about.

48
00:02:02,008 --> 00:02:04,270
One line runs from treat to healthy.

49
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:06,863
The other runs from messy to tiffin ready.

50
00:02:07,213 --> 00:02:10,383
Sweet shop laddoos are
tasty, but sugary and sticky.

51
00:02:10,383 --> 00:02:13,325
Chocolate bars pack well,
but they are a treat.

52
00:02:13,675 --> 00:02:16,553
Fruit is healthy, but it
bruises in a school bag.

53
00:02:16,903 --> 00:02:19,453
The healthy, tiffin ready corner is empty.

54
00:02:19,453 --> 00:02:22,065
That is the gap, and Laddoo Lab moves in.

55
00:02:22,415 --> 00:02:25,451
Then Zoya writes her
position in one sentence.

56
00:02:25,801 --> 00:02:30,417
For busy parents who want a healthy
tiffin treat, Laddoo Lab is the millet

57
00:02:30,417 --> 00:02:33,144
laddoo kids love, with no refined sugar.

58
00:02:33,794 --> 00:02:36,144
Where do we see STP at work today?

59
00:02:36,494 --> 00:02:37,394
Segment.

60
00:02:37,227 --> 00:02:41,883
A bank splits its customers into
students, workers and retired people.

61
00:02:42,233 --> 00:02:43,133
Target.

62
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:47,164
An airline designs business class
for people who fly for work.

63
00:02:47,514 --> 00:02:48,414
Position.

64
00:02:48,289 --> 00:02:52,113
A food delivery app promises
one thing above all: speed.

65
00:02:52,463 --> 00:02:53,363
Review.

66
00:02:53,143 --> 00:02:58,172
A phone maker asks its users again
every year, because needs keep changing.

67
00:02:58,822 --> 00:03:00,452
But STP has a price.

68
00:03:00,452 --> 00:03:03,586
Choosing one group means
saying no to others.

69
00:03:03,936 --> 00:03:05,014
The good side.

70
00:03:05,014 --> 00:03:07,903
Focus saves money, and
the message is clear.

71
00:03:08,253 --> 00:03:09,325
The hard side.

72
00:03:09,325 --> 00:03:12,950
A group can be too small,
and labels can box people in.

73
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:14,597
Arjun is worried.

74
00:03:14,597 --> 00:03:16,521
Gym-goers still buy from us.

75
00:03:16,521 --> 00:03:18,142
Do we just drop them?

76
00:03:18,492 --> 00:03:19,628
Zoya says no.

77
00:03:19,628 --> 00:03:21,249
Anyone can still buy.

78
00:03:21,249 --> 00:03:23,627
We just design for parents first.

79
00:03:23,977 --> 00:03:24,877
The lesson?

80
00:03:24,828 --> 00:03:27,675
Pick a focus, but keep
listening to everyone.

81
00:03:28,325 --> 00:03:29,365
Quick check.

82
00:03:29,365 --> 00:03:33,589
Zoya sorts buyers into parents,
gym-goers and office snackers.

83
00:03:33,589 --> 00:03:35,637
Which step of STP is this?

84
00:03:37,537 --> 00:03:38,497
Segmentation!

85
00:03:38,497 --> 00:03:41,939
She splits the market into
groups with similar needs.

86
00:03:42,539 --> 00:03:43,548
So, remember.

87
00:03:43,848 --> 00:03:47,151
Segment: split the market into
groups with similar needs.

88
00:03:47,501 --> 00:03:49,921
Target: choose the group
you can serve best.

89
00:03:50,271 --> 00:03:53,462
Position: own one clear,
different place in their minds.

90
00:03:54,062 --> 00:03:58,422
Next time, we'll build the offer
itself, with the marketing mix and its

91
00:03:58,422 --> 00:03:59,322
four Ps.
